															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/acog" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5343188); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5343188&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605838667" alt="ACoG" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5343188)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/acog" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5343188); return false;" >ACoG</a></span> <span class="odate time_1558153013 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 May 2019 04:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4252845">
									

<p>My only complaint is that the addendums containing exploration/recovery logs read less like transcripts and more like scripts. Like for a play. There's description where there doesn't need to be (it is not usually relevant to the mission or the SCP when a task force member <em>laughs</em>), and insufficient description in other places (those Samsara guys sure do <em>pause</em> a lot, instead of, say, <em>take a moment to survey their surroundings</em>, or something similarly more detailed).</p>
<p>Continuing with that second point, there are so, SO many spots where you repeat verbs way too many times in quick succession. <em>Pauses</em>, <em>motions</em>, etc.</p>
<p>You also tend to stray away from the &quot;toneless/impartial&quot; inflection I would expect from an in-universe SCP employee typing up an actual transcript. I don't really know how to describe it better than that. I'm talking about your narrative scene/event descriptions, not your characters' dialogue. It causes the addendums to lose a certain sense of professionalism.</p>
<p>With all that in mind, I did like this enough to re-read it. It's good overall.</p>

							</div>

								
					
